.TH ENC 1 "openssl 3.0" "2026-03-02" "Zephyr base toolchain"
.SH NAME
enc \- symmetric cipher command of the OpenSSL toolkit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openssl enc
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B enc
command seals or unseals data at the byte level using a symmetric block or
stream cipher selected with a cipher option. Cipher options begin with a
single leading dash, then the name \fBaes\fR, a key-width of 128, 192 or 256,
a sub-dash, and the mode name.
.SH ENCRYPTION MODE OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-aes-128-cbc
AES-128 in Cipher Block Chaining mode. Requires padding; message length is
rounded up to a multiple of 16 bytes.
.TP
.BR \-aes-256-cbc
AES-256 Cipher Block Chaining mode. Adds padding bytes when encrypting.
.TP
.BR \-aes-256-ctr
AES-256 Counter mode. A streaming mode: the plaintext is XORed with a
keystream generated from the counter, so the ciphertext has the \fBexact
same length\fR as the plaintext and no padding is added. Decryption performs
the identical XOR operation, so \fBenc -d\fR with this mode recovers the
cipher plaintext unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-aes-256-gcm
AES-256 Galois/Counter mode with authentication tag.
.SH DECRYPTION
Decryption uses the same mode option together with a leading
.B \-d
. Write the mode you intend to use as an explicit option token.
.SH KEY AND IV
The key and initialisation vector, when supplied, are given as hex text after
.B \-K
and
.B \-iv
respectively. The AES counter mode cipher accepts a 256-bit key and a 128-bit
IV.
.SH NOTES
When every in-task byte must be reproduced verbatim (for example a handoff
message sealed without padding), use the streaming counter mode, because only
streaming modes preserve the message length and recover the plaintext with
the same operation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1)